WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.501 --> 00:00:03.253
Hi. I'm Antoine Fuqua,
the director of The Guilty.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:15.140
911. Emergency operator 625.

00:00:15.224 --> 00:00:17.434
What is the address
of the emergency?

00:00:17.518 --> 00:00:19.978
-Hi, sweetie.
-Sorry, you called 911.

00:00:20.062 --> 00:00:22.731
One of my favourite scenes
to film

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:25.651
would probably be when
Riley calls in, the actress.

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:27.402
It's the first time
she says:

00:00:27.486 --> 00:00:28.904
"I want to talk, sweetie."

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:30.405
While he's dealing with

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:32.908
a woman that's obviously
been kidnapped

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:34.910
and is in danger,

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:36.370
his phone is ringing.

00:00:36.954 --> 00:00:38.622
And his little girl's
on the phone.

00:00:39.498 --> 00:00:42.960
And he's trying to
make a decision

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:45.170
to talk to his little girl,
who he's very close to,

00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:46.296
but he's divorced.

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:48.924
And, at the same time,
a reporter calls in.

00:00:49.007 --> 00:00:50.092
This is Joe.

00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:51.134
Joe, hey.

00:00:51.218 --> 00:00:52.803
Do you have time to answer
a couple of questions

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:53.804
about tomorrow?

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:55.180
Who is this?

00:00:55.264 --> 00:00:56.807
Are you a journalist?

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.141
All this is kind of
happening

00:00:58.225 --> 00:00:59.476
while he's dealing with

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.937
a kidnapped woman
that's come in.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.399
How do you desperately
help someone that's in need?

00:01:05.482 --> 00:01:06.942
How do you have
the discipline

00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:08.610
not to pick up the phone
when your little girl,

00:01:08.694 --> 00:01:10.112
when you've been trying to
call her all day,

00:01:10.195 --> 00:01:12.030
your ex-wife won't let you
speak to her…

00:01:12.114 --> 00:01:14.074
And then you have
a reporter calling you

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:16.410
wanting to know
your side of the story

00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:18.203
of something you did wrong.

00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:19.872
To me,
that's a complex scene.

00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:21.957
And, in filming that scene,

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.584
I had to restrain myself

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.961
because there's a tendency
to want to do

00:01:26.044 --> 00:01:27.296
a lot of things
with the camera.

00:01:27.379 --> 00:01:30.299
The discipline for me was
not to move the camera.

00:01:30.966 --> 00:01:32.885
To just be there with him

00:01:32.968 --> 00:01:34.928
and let the actor do it.

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:39.266
I told Jake: "I'm going to
stay wide for a lot of it."

00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:40.559
"And then
I'm just going to march in."

00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:42.519
"Closer, closer, closer."

00:01:42.603 --> 00:01:43.812
To make the audience,
sort of,

00:01:43.896 --> 00:01:45.731
have some distance
from what's happening.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:47.191
Until she calls.

00:01:47.274 --> 00:01:49.443
And, once she calls,
I go really close.

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:50.485
Right in here

00:01:50.569 --> 00:01:53.071
and you can see his eyes
darting back and forth

00:01:53.155 --> 00:01:56.116
as he's trying to process
what the woman's saying.

00:01:56.742 --> 00:01:57.618
Who is that?

00:01:58.577 --> 00:02:00.913
-Yes, I understand.
-Is there someone with you?

00:02:02.581 --> 00:02:06.084
As he's trying to register
in his brain visually

00:02:06.168 --> 00:02:08.045
what's happening to her,

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:10.923
the audience starts to do
the same

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:13.800
and it makes you
lean in more to listen

00:02:13.884 --> 00:02:15.010
to what's being said.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:22.809
They're not in the room
with me.

00:02:22.893 --> 00:02:24.019
They're on Zoom.

00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:26.271
They're in whatever world
they're in, right?

00:02:26.355 --> 00:02:29.233
So, they would call in.
We had specific times.

00:02:29.316 --> 00:02:31.735
For actors, we're going to
do 20 minutes today.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:34.488
And I can see them
in their homes

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:35.906
and wherever they were.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:37.658
Jake could never see them.

00:02:37.741 --> 00:02:39.910
And so, they would get
their cue from me

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.120
and then they would go
into their performance

00:02:42.204 --> 00:02:43.789
as he answered the call.

00:02:44.414 --> 00:02:47.543
But the actors that called
in were amazing:

00:02:47.626 --> 00:02:49.837
Ethan Hawke
and Peter Sarsgaard

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.381
and Riley and Da'Vine.

00:02:52.464 --> 00:02:53.340
It's just, like,

00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:54.967
a blessing to have
those kinds of actors.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.260
Paul Dano…

00:02:56.343 --> 00:02:59.346
Their performance makes
Jake's performance better

00:02:59.972 --> 00:03:01.473
because,
if he doesn't believe them,

00:03:01.557 --> 00:03:02.683
we don't believe them.

00:03:03.308 --> 00:03:05.686
Most of us have been there
before in some way

00:03:05.769 --> 00:03:07.688
where you can't get people
to do

00:03:07.771 --> 00:03:09.189
what you want over the phone

00:03:09.273 --> 00:03:10.774
and you can't do anything
about it.

00:03:10.858 --> 00:03:14.236
An emotional person like Joe
who doesn't really do this,

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.364
he's a street cop,
is not used to that rhythm.

00:03:17.447 --> 00:03:18.365
Shit.

00:03:18.866 --> 00:03:21.285
He doesn't know how to deal
with all these emotions.

00:03:21.368 --> 00:03:23.453
-Fuck me?
-Fuck you! Go to hell.

00:03:23.537 --> 00:03:24.830
Alright, yeah. Fuck you!

00:03:27.165 --> 00:03:28.876
And he's expecting everybody
to move

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:30.169
when he wants them to move

00:03:30.252 --> 00:03:32.045
and get helicopters in there
and do all these things

00:03:32.129 --> 00:03:33.881
and that's just
not the way it works.

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:36.300
So that builds tension.

00:03:37.384 --> 00:03:39.678
It's frustrating,
a bit, as a director

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:42.848
because, you know, you're
a slave to the technology.

00:03:42.931 --> 00:03:45.434
You know, so, if the Zoom
glitches, which it did,

00:03:45.517 --> 00:03:48.520
if someone down the street
is making noise…

00:03:48.604 --> 00:03:49.521
In New York City,

00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:51.648
while someone's doing
their side of things,

00:03:51.732 --> 00:03:53.108
you can't control
any of that.

00:03:53.192 --> 00:03:54.526
Glitches happening…

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.528
So, anything that
you were feeling,

00:03:57.112 --> 00:03:58.405
especially Jake,

00:03:58.488 --> 00:04:00.365
would wind up on the screen.

00:04:00.449 --> 00:04:01.575
He would get…

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:03.952
his own voice in his ear.

00:04:04.494 --> 00:04:07.122
And he would just sometimes,
just:

00:04:07.998 --> 00:04:09.958
"I can't…
This is driving me crazy."

00:04:10.042 --> 00:04:11.835
And I would keep the camera
rolling

00:04:13.045 --> 00:04:14.922
and he'd kind of
get back into it again

00:04:15.005 --> 00:04:17.382
and some of that's in
the cut because it was real.

00:04:28.435 --> 00:04:29.311
You know,

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:32.564
I always use lighting
as another character.

00:04:32.648 --> 00:04:34.816
The city itself is
a character, you know.

00:04:35.651 --> 00:04:37.694
The first shot of the sunset

00:04:38.237 --> 00:04:39.905
with L.A. on fire,

00:04:39.988 --> 00:04:42.366
for me, is Dante's Inferno

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:44.201
and it sets off an urgency.

00:04:44.952 --> 00:04:47.704
The lighting on the inside
when we first meet Joe…

00:04:47.788 --> 00:04:50.415
It's daytime. It's sort of
a soft glow light.

00:04:50.499 --> 00:04:52.042
Sort of like purgatory.

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:53.460
It's a non-lighting.

00:04:53.544 --> 00:04:55.629
It sort of just
floats there, right?

00:04:57.339 --> 00:04:59.383
Later, as it gets darker,

00:04:59.466 --> 00:05:00.926
when you see
the Alka-Seltzer

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.136
glass

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.305
when he comes
from calling his daughter,

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:07.224
it's darker
when he goes back in.

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:10.102
So, subconsciously
when you see that way

00:05:10.769 --> 00:05:11.937
towards the blinds,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.565
it's now darker than
what you saw before.

00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:15.524
But, at the same time,

00:05:15.607 --> 00:05:16.859
the subject matter's getting
darker

00:05:16.942 --> 00:05:18.902
because she's now called in.

00:05:18.986 --> 00:05:21.280
911. Emergency operator 625.

00:05:21.363 --> 00:05:22.739
What is the address
of the emergency?

00:05:22.823 --> 00:05:26.660
And, every time an audience
sees the red light,

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:28.161
red is violence.

00:05:28.245 --> 00:05:29.788
That just feels urgent.

00:05:29.872 --> 00:05:32.249
So, those big close-ups
of the red light were

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:34.334
to make you feel
that urgency.

00:05:34.418 --> 00:05:35.794
When he goes
into the other room,

00:05:35.878 --> 00:05:36.962
that whole room is dark

00:05:37.045 --> 00:05:38.881
except for
the little pin light

00:05:38.964 --> 00:05:41.133
because his character's
getting darker.

00:05:41.216 --> 00:05:42.134
Right?

00:05:42.217 --> 00:05:44.303
The beginning of the movie
when he's in the bathroom is

00:05:44.386 --> 00:05:47.264
all white because it's
the beginning of purgatory.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:48.390
You're in the clouds.

00:05:49.183 --> 00:05:50.726
He's not quite anywhere.

00:05:51.226 --> 00:05:52.519
But he's suffering.

00:05:52.603 --> 00:05:53.812
When he goes back
in the bathroom,

00:05:53.896 --> 00:05:55.147
he's kind of green.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.899
The concept is like a snake

00:05:56.982 --> 00:05:59.484
just getting tighter
and tighter and tighter

00:05:59.568 --> 00:06:02.404
and we're watching
a man dying, right?

00:06:02.487 --> 00:06:03.947
Because he's guilty

00:06:04.031 --> 00:06:05.741
and he's hiding it.

00:06:05.824 --> 00:06:07.743
But it's coming
out of his body

00:06:07.826 --> 00:06:10.162
and he's coughing and it's
starting to ooze out of him.

00:06:10.245 --> 00:06:11.371
He can't escape it.

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:16.835
And, if you look at the man,
he's very sweaty

00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:18.921
and he looks pale.

00:06:20.047 --> 00:06:21.590
And a little green.

00:06:22.466 --> 00:06:23.800
He looks like death.

00:06:27.513 --> 00:06:30.224
I had a list of things I was
going to possibly shoot

00:06:30.307 --> 00:06:33.310
to take you to some of the
worlds that you're hearing.

00:06:34.311 --> 00:06:36.939
And then,
when I cut it together,

00:06:37.022 --> 00:06:39.983
it would completely
go against what the film is.

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:42.277
The audience imagination
is way stronger

00:06:42.361 --> 00:06:44.446
than anything that
I designed to shoot.

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.240
People created that story.

00:06:46.823 --> 00:06:48.867
I learnt to trust that more,

00:06:48.951 --> 00:06:50.994
to trust an audience
imagination more.

00:06:51.954 --> 00:06:53.872
Yeah, I think
that's the biggest thing.

00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:56.375
Thank you for watching
The Guilty.

00:06:56.458 --> 00:06:57.543
Only on Netflix.

